Reflection on Sixth Iteration BBO Strategy

As my BBO capstone project progressed to the sixth iteration with 15 accumulated data points, I started to think about optimisation less as isolated guesses and more as a process of progressive understanding. This shift closely mirrors how CNNs build increasingly abstract representations from raw input. Early iterations of my strategy were focused on broad exploration, similar to how early convolutional layers detect simple features like edges or textures. As more data became available, I began refining queries around regions that showed promising responses, analogous to deeper CNN layers combining low-level features into higher-level patterns. This mindset helped me justify narrowing my search space while still preserving enough diversity to avoid premature convergence.

The historical impact of LeNet and subsequent CNN architectures also resonates with the incremental improvements in my BBO project. CNN breakthroughs were not the result of a single radical change, but of many small, well-motivated refinements in architecture, training procedures, and data usage. Similarly, no single iteration in my BBO process dramatically transformed performance. Instead, each round modestly improved my understanding of the objective functions, reduced uncertainty, and informed better-targeted queries. Viewing progress as cumulative rather than instantaneous made the slower gains of later iterations feel meaningful rather than stagnant.

A key challenge in both CNN training and BBO optimisation is balancing competing trade-offs. In CNNs, increasing depth can improve representational power but also raises computational cost and overfitting risk. I faced a parallel decision when choosing between exploration and exploitation. Wide exploration increased coverage of the input space but diluted query efficiency, while aggressive exploitation risked overfitting to local optima based on limited data. By iteration six, I leaned slightly more toward exploitation but still reserved some query diversity as a safeguard — much like using regularisation or validation data to control overfitting in deep networks.

Several CNN concepts helped me think differently about how my optimisation model learns from accumulated data. Pooling, in particular, influenced my thinking: just as pooling layers reduce sensitivity to small spatial variations, I began focusing more on broader performance trends rather than reacting strongly to individual noisy observations. Loss functions also offered a useful analogy. Instead of optimising purely for immediate performance gains, I evaluated queries based on how much information they contributed to improving the overall model of the function. This reframing helped me prioritise learning efficiency over short-term rewards.

Finally, reflecting on the interview with Andrea Dunbar and the realities of deploying CNNs in edge AI systems encouraged me to think more carefully about how I benchmark success in my BBO project. In real-world deployments, the “best” model is often not the most complex, but the one that balances accuracy, robustness, and resource constraints. Applying this perspective, I evaluated my BBO strategy not only by how close it came to optimal values, but by how efficiently it reached them given a limited query budget. This reinforced the idea that optimisation success should be measured holistically, considering both performance and practicality.

Overall, framing my BBO strategy through the lens of CNN design and deployment helped me better understand the trade-offs inherent in optimisation and guided more intentional decision-making as the project matured.
